Independence of implementation
------------------------------
This project contains no code from any other project. It was written from a
functional brief: which categories of local Codex state are worth reporting on,
and which are safe to move aside.

The categories themselves were informed by "keep-codex-fast"
(https://github.com/vibeforge1111/keep-codex-fast), an MIT-licensed tool with an
overlapping purpose. Functional ideas are not subject to copyright, and because
no source text, structure, or documentation was copied from it, that project's
MIT notice does not need to travel with this one. It is credited here as a
courtesy, not as a license obligation.

Where this implementation deliberately differs:

  * operations are journalled before execution and reversible afterwards, with a
    single `restore` command instead of generated per-run scripts
  * database updates derive their undo values from live rows at execution time
  * every task declares the schema it requires and is skipped with a stated
    reason when Codex ships something different
  * the config.toml rewrite is validated by re-parsing and diffing before it is
    allowed to run
  * "is Codex running" is answered by which process holds the databases open,
    rather than by matching process names
  * scan is read-only by construction and covered by a test asserting the Codex
    home is unchanged

Third-party dependencies
------------------------
None. The Python standard library only.

